1. Open the Local Security Policy tool on your Windows Server and configure a password policy that requires at least 8 characters, includes uppercase and lowercase letters, and enforces password changes every 45 days.
==>Press Win + R, type secpol.msc, and press Enter to open Local Security Policy.
Expand Account Policies → Password Policy.
Configure these settings (double-click each one):
Minimum password length → set to 8 characters.
Password must meet complexity requirements → Enabled.
Maximum password age → set to 45 days.
Optionally strengthen further:
Enforce password history → 24 (or higher).
Minimum password age → 1 day (prevents immediate reuse cycling).
Click OK on each dialog. Changes take effect for new password changes.


2. Set up an account lockout policy using the Local Security Policy tool so that a user account is locked after 3 invalid login attempts and remains locked for 15 minutes before it can be used again.
=>In the same Local Security Policy console, expand Account Policies → Account Lockout Policy.
Configure:
Account lockout threshold → 3 invalid logon attempts.
Account lockout duration → 15 minutes.
Reset account lockout counter after → 15 minutes.
When you set the threshold, Windows will prompt you to configure the other two values; accept or adjust them to 15 minutes.
Click OK.

3. Enable and configure audit log settings in the Local Security Policy tool to track successful and failed logon attempts on your server.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Look for 'Audit logon events' under Local Policies → Audit Policy.</em>
=>In Local Security Policy, expand Local Policies → Audit Policy.
Double-click Audit logon events.
Check both Success and Failure.
Click OK.

4. Harden your Windows Server by disabling at least two unnecessary services (for example: Print Spooler, Remote Registry) using the Services management console, and explain in a short note why you chose these services.
=>Press Win + R, type services.msc, and press Enter.
Locate and double-click each service, set Startup type to Disabled, and click Stop if it is running:
Print Spooler (Spooler)
Remote Registry (RemoteRegistry)
Click OK.

5. Submit a report (1-2 pages) describing all the security policies you configured, including screenshots of your password policy, account lockout policy, and audit log settings. Briefly explain how each policy helps improve server security.

